  "diagnosis_text": "The slow transfer is reproducible: h1\u2192h5 achieved about 0.20 Mbit/s, with ping RTT averaging 204 ms. The path is h1\u2013s4\u2013s2\u2013s1\u2013s3\u2013s6\u2013h5. So far, s2\u2019s port toward s1 shows queued traffic and repeated rate-limit events; I\u2019m checking the remaining path and comparison traffic to confirm the bottleneck.**The bottleneck is localised to s2 port 1 transmitting toward s1 port 1.** Measurements strongly indicate an egress rate limit near **1 Mbit/s**, with queueing and ongoing traffic sharing that capacity.\n\nEvidence:\n- **h1\u2192h5:** TCP throughput **0.20 Mbit/s**; ping averaged **204 ms** (134\u2013297 ms), with no loss in five probes.\n- **s2\u2192s1:** Two separate samples showed approximately **1.00 Mbit/s** transmitted, **24\u201334 KB queued**, and **353\u2013366 queue-overlimit events per two-second sample**. No packet drops or interface errors were recorded.\n- Other sampled forward-path ports showed **no backlog or overlimit events**.\n- Comparison transfers avoiding the central links were fast: **h1\u2192h3 \u224832.2 Gbit/s** and **h7\u2192h5 \u224833.3 Gbit/s** in this virtual network. These exercise the source-side and destination-side access paths respectively.\n\nThe affected path is:\n`h1 \u2192 s4 \u2192 s2 \u2192 s1 \u2192 s3 \u2192 s6 \u2192 h5`\n\nTraffic near 1 Mbit/s persisted after the bounded test ended, suggesting other ongoing traffic contributes to contention; its source is not established.\n\n**Next step:** Inspect the Linux traffic-control/qdisc and any OVS QoS configuration on **s2-eth1**, especially rate and queue limits, and identify traffic sharing that egress. The counters support a shaping bottleneck, but the exact configured policy is not exposed by these tools. No changes were made.",
